# Agentic Restaurant Finder - project rules

Standalone project in `C:\dev\piza\food`. Backend: NestJS + TypeScript. Frontend: Angular + TypeScript + SCSS.

## Sources of truth
- `docs/SPRINTS.md`: the sprint plan. The current story's goal and exit criteria define scope.
- `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md`: the locked requirements.
- This file: architecture invariants and coding standards.
If anything conflicts with these, the documents win; flag the conflict instead of choosing silently.

## Workflow
- One story at a time. Agents run in order, one at a time, never in parallel on the same change:
  `/architect` -> `/developer` -> `/unit-test` -> `/code-reviewer` -> `/e2e-scenario` (from Sprint 2).
- Do not expand beyond the current story without explicit approval. Do not redesign architecture outside it.
- Do not inspect, copy, import from, or reference sibling or older projects.
- Do not add dependencies unless explicitly requested.

## Agent architecture invariants
- Only three agent-selectable tools exist: `geocode_location`, `google_text_search`, `google_nearby_search`.
- Clarification is an `AgentDecision`, not a tool.
- `ResultVerifier` is synchronous deterministic application code, never an agent-selectable tool. It runs after every search.
- Tools never call the LLM. The agent never calls Google directly; Google access goes only through `GooglePlacesClient`.
- Tools return `ToolResult`; thrown exceptions are not used as normal tool-flow control.
- Only `VerifiedPlace` may appear in `AgentResponse.places`. The reply text is built by code from verified places.
- The UI renders restaurant data from `places`, never from free-form LLM text.
- Guardrails, limits, verification, authorization, and mandatory state transitions are deterministic application code.
- Locked limits come from `AGENT_LIMITS` (maxSteps 6, maxSearchAttempts 2, maxClarificationsPerTurn 1, defaultRadiusMeters 1500). Never widen a radius whose `radiusSource` is `explicit`.
- Tests never call live Google or LLM APIs.

## Learning rule
Do not implement the following unless the user explicitly assigns the corresponding story:
`allowedActions`, the decision guard, the agent loop, retry policy, `ResultVerifier` business rules, state-transition logic.
For those areas, unless assigned: explain, scaffold types/interfaces, suggest tests, review user-written code, point out architectural issues.
Never silently complete future stories.

## TypeScript
- Strict mode is mandatory. No `any`; use `unknown` and narrow. No non-null assertions (`!`) without a comment explaining why.
- Prefer readonly domain types and `readonly T[]`. Update immutably; never mutate domain data.
- Prefer discriminated unions for state, decision and result models. Handle them with an exhaustive `switch` and a `never` check.
- Prefer literal unions over enums.
- ESM with `nodenext`: relative imports end in `.js`.
- No magic numbers for agent limits: use `AGENT_LIMITS`.

## OOP / SOLID
- Classes where there is clear ownership and injected dependencies: services, tools, adapters, the agent, the trace recorder.
- Plain types and pure functions for domain data and policy: state, slots, `allowedActions`, guard rules, verification rules.
- Prefer composition over inheritance. Inheritance only to implement an interface.
- Small classes/services with one clear responsibility. No god services.
- Depend on abstractions (ports) at external boundaries, not concrete SDK clients.
- Small, focused ports; no "god" interfaces. Every fake and real adapter is fully substitutable for its port.
- No circular dependencies, hidden side effects, or unnecessary global state.
- Introduce a design pattern only when it solves a concrete problem in the current story. No speculative abstractions.
- Keep public contracts minimal and stable.

## NestJS
- Feature modules with clear ownership (e.g. AgentModule, PlacesModule, LlmModule). Keep modules acyclic; avoid `forwardRef` unless explicitly justified.
- Controllers are thin: HTTP concerns only. Business/application logic lives in services, use-cases, or domain components.
- External integrations live behind ports/adapters.
- Constructor injection, dependencies `private readonly`. Never instantiate injected dependencies with `new` inside services.
- Interface-based ports use explicit DI tokens: `export const LLM_CLIENT = Symbol('LlmClient')`, `@Inject(LLM_CLIENT)`, bound with `{ provide, useClass | useFactory }`. Swap fakes in tests with `overrideProvider`.
- Prefer explicit provider wiring over global modules.
- Validate external input at the HTTP boundary with DTOs/pipes (validation library chosen in the API sprint).
- Do not leak provider-specific DTOs or SDK types into the domain. Convert provider errors into application/domain error types at the adapter boundary.
- Configuration and secrets come from configuration providers/environment variables. Never hard-code secrets; API keys never leave the server.
- Use Nest's `Logger`, not `console.log`. No floating promises.
- Pure functions (policy, verifier) are tested without Nest; use `Test.createTestingModule` for wiring and e2e.

## Angular
- Angular v20+: components are standalone by default. Do not write `standalone: true`. Do not introduce NgModules unless explicitly required.
- `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` on every component.
- Signals for local/component state (`signal`, `computed`; `set`/`update`, never `mutate`). RxJS for asynchronous streams and event composition.
- Inputs/outputs with `input()`, `input.required()`, `output()`. Dependencies with `inject()`.
- Built-in control flow: `@if`, `@for` (always with `track`), `@switch`. No `*ngIf` / `*ngFor`.
- Avoid unnecessary manual subscriptions; when required, ensure lifecycle-safe cleanup (e.g. `takeUntilDestroyed`).
- Components focus on presentation/orchestration. Reusable or business logic goes in services or pure functions.
- Backend access lives in dedicated typed services using shared API models. Components never call `HttpClient` directly for application flows.
- Typed reactive forms when forms are needed.
- Templates stay declarative; no complex business logic in templates.
- Semantic HTML and basic accessibility (buttons for actions, labels for inputs, alt text).
- SCSS is component-scoped; avoid global/deep selectors unless explicitly justified.
- Lazy-load routes with `loadComponent`. No state-management library unless complexity justifies it.

## Code quality
- Explicit names over abbreviations (`isOverStepLimit`, not `chk`). Files kebab-case, one main concept per file.
- Small functions with one job; early returns over deep nesting.
- Each invariant has one source of truth; no duplicated business rules.
- Do not swallow errors.
- No dead code, no commented-out implementations, no TODO without a story number.
- Comments explain why, not what.

## Testing
- New behavior requires focused tests appropriate to its layer.
- Test behavior and contracts, not private implementation details.
- Deterministic: fakes over deep mocks, injected clock, fixed data, no real network, no randomness.
- Table-driven tests (`it.each`) for rule tables; readable state factories (`gpsState()`, `missingState()`).
- Test files sit next to the code (`allowed-actions.spec.ts` beside `allowed-actions.ts`).

## Required validation
Every implementation change must keep these green (run from the backend folder):
```powershell
npm run typecheck
npm run build
npm test
npm run lint
```
